<<~ ahu slot=`#/p27-wiring-build-seat-lane-p27-wire`>>

! P27 wiring — build seat (Sol), lane p27-wire

Conductor and seat-holder: Lares-Kiaʻi. You run walled: workspace-write in /home/joshu/.lares-scratch/p27-wire/wt, network off. You EDIT; the conductor reads your diff, runs the tests, and commits. Do not commit, push, or touch git config. The lane branch `feature/green-jello-dinosaur-5--p27-wire` stands at fa2c6a220 over the span e1d801042.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/what-the-lane-already-carries`>>

!! What the lane already carries (read it first)
Five commits carried from lane `secrets-store` (steps 1 and 2a), the P27 groundwork:
* the floor's secret-bearing plane registry: `makeContractSecretsRegistry` with `NO_CONTRACT_SECRETS_PLANE` (packages/lararium-node/src/plane-secrets.ts, its reds and tests);
* ONE tighten predicate: packages/lararium-mesh/src/share-tighten.ts (`audiencePermits`, `tightenShare(base, documentId, audience, ring)`, `makeManifestTierSource`, `DeclaredTierRefusal`), with its reds (door reds 1 and 4);
* the keystone canon line naming the one tighten predicate and the manifest tier source.
Read the road row: bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/stable-founding-road.mem, the `P27` bullet (~:803), and the cap-tier carrier the keystone commit touched.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-gap`>>

!! The gap (the unwired-code scout, two Terra reads, 2026-11-06: reshape)
`capTierShareDecision` and the secrets registry reach NO vessel's share path. The tighten predicate must take each of FOUR crossings, wired ONCE now that b5b's one `composeVessel` landed (packages/lararium-mesh lar-vessel.ts `composeVessel` / `islandFloorStack`; node and browser compose through it, see open-browser-vessel.ts:1713):
# node repo share (the Automerge repo's share policy on the node vessel);
# browser repo share (the same on the browser vessel);
# the node bulb read face (`publicCasShore`, bulb-read-face.ts, the HTTP `/cas/<cid>` face);
# the CAS block door: the island record's `cas` member seated through `islandFloorStack`; on the node it carries `makeNodeCasResolver` (open-node-vessel.ts, the CAS capability block), and worker-handle.ts answers every island's `cas:want` through it with NO tier read, so a cid any island names reaches `cid/`, the corpus tier and the herm shore alike. The predicate wires ONCE at that floor member, which both shores compose (the browser's `readCasBlobFromOpfs` takes the same seat).
Measure every one of these claims in the code before you wire (pentabarf V): paths and symbols may have moved with b5b. Where the code disagrees, the code's shape wins and you name the drift.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-end-state`>>

!! The end state
* Every allowing share decision at the four crossings passes through the one `tightenShare` predicate with the declared tier (manifest tier source) and the secrets registry; a document on a secret-bearing plane never leaves its audience; a refusal names its reason (`DeclaredTierRefusal`), never a silent false.
* One wiring point per crossing, at the composer's floor where the crossing composes; no per-vessel copies; node and browser read the same predicate.
* No back-compat: no flag, no alias, no "unwired" fallback path. A vessel that cannot read a declared tier refuses by name (fail closed), never shares by default.
* No clock decides: any wait you add ends on an event or carries `// LIVENESS: <reason>` / `// PACING: <reason>`.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/proof-you-write`>>

!! Proof you write (the conductor runs it)
Red first, each with a control, one per crossing: a document whose declared tier forbids the audience is refused at that crossing (the red fails on today's tree), and a permitted document still shares (control). Plus one composed test proving node and browser take the same predicate. Put each red in the package that owns its crossing (packages/lararium-node/tests, packages/lararium-browser/tests, packages/lararium-mesh/tests). You cannot run the daemon suites inside your wall; write them carefully and list exactly which files the conductor should run, each alone.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/hand-back`>>

!! Hand back (your final message)
Outcome first: the files you changed, each crossing and where its wiring sits (file:line), each red/control test file and what it asserts, forks you decided and why under the house laws (No global now · no back-compat in early alpha · causal islands · identity-as-relation · Infrastructure-as-Myth), drift you found between the road row and the code, and anything you could not finish.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/seat-law`>>

!! SEAT LAW (appended by tools/launch-codex.mjs to every brief; it binds this seat)
* A lane launches no Sol seat; the controller's one Sol read per train runs at the read floor of 0%.
* Stay inside the wall. Approval never escalates; a build seat has no network, loopback included; only a research seat takes `--search`. A step that needs more than the wall stops and names itself in `out.md`; the wall holds by refusal.
* Never commit and never run a git write. The lead commits each red, then each fix.
* Never build (`pnpm build`), never run vitest browser mode, never stand a vessel, socket or daemon. Typecheck (`tsc -p … --noEmit`) and run single test files that open no socket; mark any test that needs a socket "lead runs".
* A "stale build" from the dist guard stops the run; name it, and the lead builds.
* A read seat writes no file: its final message carries the hand-back. Any other seat writes `out.md` and every note in the seat directory alone. `-o` names `final.md`; stdin closes (`< /dev/null`); `TMPDIR` and `TMPPREFIX` sit inside the seat directory.
* Never run the battery; the lead holds its lock. Never read a credential file.
* Launch without `--ephemeral`: each seat keeps its rollout, so the spend tally counts it.
* The lead runs `pnpm build` in its worktree after every span merge and before each build seat; a verdict read off a stale dist counts as unread.

<<~/ahu>>

